IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `scr-dates`
IMPORT `scr-duties`

-- Supreme Court (General Civil Procedure) Rules 2025 (Vic) -- the PARTY's questions about starting and running a claim:
-- Order 1 (which proceedings the Rules apply to, r 1.05), Order 3 (time), Orders 4 and 5 (originating process, how long a
-- writ stays valid), Orders 6 and 7 (service, in and out of Australia, with r 8.04 on the time for appearance), Orders 13
-- and 14 (pleadings), Order 15 (persons under disability: litigation guardians, approving compromises) and Order 18A
-- (group proceedings).
--
-- SEAT: the individual who is a party to a civil proceeding in the Supreme Court of Victoria, usually the person bringing a
-- personal injury claim (and, where the Rules put the step on the other side, the individual answering it): "Do the 2025
-- Rules apply to my case?", "By when must I ...?", "Has the defendant been served?", "Am I taken to admit this?", "Must I
-- sue through a litigation guardian?", "Is my settlement valid without the Court's approval?". The Court, the Prothonotary
-- and the lawyers get no seat: their orders, directions and acts are asked as facts.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE where the Rules' own words say no ("means", "shall not",
-- "unless", "if, but only if", an "if" or "where" condition that fails, a period the Rules fix); NOTHING where the Rules do
-- not answer on the facts given, with a "NOTHING-kind" comment. Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, dates, numbers,
-- a choice from a list); the duties with a deadline are DEONTIC rules (scr-duties.l4) run inside.
-- Periods are counted by Order 3 (scr-dates.l4); where a question does not ask, the Court is taken not to have ordered
-- the Christmas vacation (24 December to 9 January) counted (r 3.04(1)); the general period question asks.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Supreme Court
-- (General Civil Procedure) Rules 2025, S.R. No. 85/2025, Version No. 003 (1 January 2026), not the authorised version.
-- (c) State of Victoria; published with the authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be
-- reproduced except in accordance with the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026
-- (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Supreme Court civil procedure: starting and running my claim`

§§ `Order 1 -- which proceedings the 2025 Rules apply to (rr 1.03 to 1.05)`

-- r 1.03(1): "These Rules come into operation on 8 September 2025." r 1.04: "commencement date means 8 September 2025".
`the commencement date` MEANS `the calendar date` 8 9 2025

-- r 1.05(1): "Subject to this Rule and to any transitional or other provision in these Rules to the contrary, these Rules
-- apply to every civil proceeding commenced in the Court whether before, on or after the commencement date." (2): "These
-- Rules do not apply to a civil proceeding to which any other Chapter of the Rules of the Supreme Court applies except as
-- that Chapter provides."
-- Not a civil proceeding in the Court: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (r 1.05 speaks only of civil proceedings
-- in the Court).
@export Do the Supreme Court (General Civil Procedure) Rules 2025 apply to my civil case in the Supreme Court of Victoria (r 1.05(1), (2))? They apply to every civil proceeding in the Court, begun before, on or after 8 September 2025, except one governed by another Chapter of the Rules of the Supreme Court.
GIVEN `is it a civil case in the Supreme Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your case is a civil proceeding in the Supreme Court of Victoria (not a criminal case, and not in another court or tribunal)
      `does another Chapter govern it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another Chapter of the Rules of the Supreme Court (not Chapter I, these Rules) applies to your case; answer no if unsure
      `does that Chapter apply these Rules` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If another Chapter applies: whether that Chapter says these Rules apply to the matter you are asking about (answer no if another Chapter does not apply)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Do the 2025 Rules apply to my case` MEANS
    IF (NOT `is it a civil case in the Supreme Court`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `does another Chapter govern it`
         THEN IF `does that Chapter apply these Rules`
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- r 1.04: "former Rules means the Supreme Court (General Civil Procedure) Rules 2015; pending proceeding means a civil
-- proceeding in the Court to which, immediately before the commencement date, the former Rules applied." r 1.05(3): "The
-- revocation of the former Rules does not affect anything done or omitted to be done in a pending proceeding and, except
-- as provided in this Part, anything so done or omitted is taken to have been done or omitted under these Rules."
-- Not a pending proceeding, or a step on or after 8 September 2025: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (r 1.05(3)
-- speaks only of what was done in a pending proceeding under the former Rules).
@export Does something done (or not done) in my case under the old 2015 Supreme Court Rules still count now that the 2025 Rules have replaced them (r 1.05(3))? In a case pending on 8 September 2025 it does, and it is taken to have been done under the 2025 Rules.
GIVEN `the day my case began` IS A DATE @desc The day your proceeding was commenced (the writ or originating motion filed), as an ISO date
      `did the 2015 Rules apply to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court (General Civil Procedure) Rules 2015 applied to your proceeding (yes for an ordinary civil claim)
      `was it still going on 7 September 2025` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding was still on foot (not finished) immediately before 8 September 2025
      `the day of the step` IS A DATE @desc The day the step was taken, or should have been taken, under the 2015 Rules
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does my step under the 2015 Rules still count` MEANS
    IF      `the day my case began` `is before` `the commencement date`
        AND `did the 2015 Rules apply to it`
        AND `was it still going on 7 September 2025`
        AND `the day of the step` `is before` `the commencement date`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Order 3 -- time (rr 3.01, 3.02, 3.04, 3.06)`

DECLARE `Unit of a period` IS ONE OF
    `days`
    `months`
    `years`

-- rr 3.01, 3.04, 3.08 (see scr-dates.l4).
@export What is the last day of a period fixed by the Supreme Court Rules, or by a judgment, order or document in my case (rr 3.01, 3.04, 3.08)? The day it runs from is left out; the Christmas vacation (24 December to 9 January) is left out unless the Court orders otherwise; a period of 5 days or less leaves out weekends; a last day on a weekend or public holiday moves to the next day the Court's office is open.
GIVEN `the day the period runs from` IS A DATE @desc The day of the event the period runs from (for example the day a document was served), as an ISO date
      `how long the period is` IS A NUMBER @desc The length of the period, as a whole number (for example 30)
      `the unit` IS A `Unit of a period` @desc Whether the period is in days, months or years
      `did the Court order the vacation counted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered that the days from 24 December to 9 January count in this period (answer no if unsure)
      `is that last day a public holiday` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the day the period would otherwise end on is a public holiday in Victoria (answer no if unsure; the encoding moves past Saturdays and Sundays itself)
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the last day of my period` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` `the last day` `is that last day a public holiday`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the unit`
            WHEN `days` THEN `the last counted day of` `how long the period is` `days after` `the day the period runs from` `did the Court order the vacation counted`
            WHEN `months` THEN `the last counted day of` `how long the period is` `months after` `the day the period runs from` `did the Court order the vacation counted`
            WHEN `years` THEN `the last counted day of` (`how long the period is` TIMES 12) `months after` `the day the period runs from` `did the Court order the vacation counted`

-- r 3.02(3): "Unless the Court otherwise orders, any time fixed by these Rules or by any order fixing, extending or
-- abridging time may be extended by consent without an order of the Court." (1), (2): the Court may extend or abridge,
-- before or after the time expires.
-- A time fixed otherwise (by an Act, or a judgment): NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Can the other parties and I agree to extend a time fixed by the Supreme Court Rules, without asking the Court (r 3.02(3))? Yes, unless the Court has ordered otherwise; the Court itself may extend or shorten a time, before or after it runs out.
GIVEN `is the time fixed by the Rules or by an order about time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time is one fixed by these Rules, or by a court order fixing, extending or shortening time (not a time fixed by an Act, such as a limitation period)
      `has the Court ordered that it cannot be extended by consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered otherwise (that the time may not be extended by agreement)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the time be extended by consent` MEANS
    IF (NOT `is the time fixed by the Rules or by an order about time`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `has the Court ordered that it cannot be extended by consent`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- r 3.06: "Where a year or more has elapsed since any party has taken any step in a proceeding, any party desiring the
-- proceeding to continue shall give to every other party not less than one month's notice in writing of that party's
-- desire." A year has elapsed once the last day of the year (counted by Order 3) has passed (fork F6).
-- A year not yet elapsed: JUST FALSE (the "where" condition fails; ruling (b) of 2026-11-05).
@export After a year or more with no step taken by anyone in my case, must I give every other party at least one month's written notice that I want to go on before my next step (r 3.06)? If nobody does, the Prothonotary may move to dismiss the case (r 25.08).
GIVEN `the day of the last step by any party` IS A DATE @desc The day any party last took a step in the proceeding, as an ISO date
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you plan to take your next step
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I give a month's notice before going on` MEANS
    IF `today` `is after` (`the last counted day of` 12 `months after` `the day of the last step by any party` FALSE)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 3.06 "not less than one month's notice": the step may be taken on the last day of the month counted from the day the
-- notice was given (that day excluded, r 3.01(2)), and after.
@export If I must give a month's notice that I want my inactive case to go on, what is the earliest day I may take my next step (rr 3.06, 3.01)?
GIVEN `the day I gave the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave every other party written notice that you want the proceeding to continue, as an ISO date
GIVETH A DATE
`The earliest day for my next step after the notice` MEANS
    `the last counted day of` 1 `months after` `the day I gave the notice` FALSE

§§ `Orders 4 and 5 -- the originating process`

DECLARE `How the proceeding is commenced` IS ONE OF
    `by writ`
    `by originating motion`
    `by writ, or by originating motion if I choose`

-- r 4.01: "Except where otherwise provided by or under any Act or these Rules a proceeding in the Court shall be commenced
-- by writ or by originating motion." r 4.04: "Except as provided by Rules 1.12, 4.05 and 4.06 and Order 58, every
-- proceeding shall be commenced by writ." r 4.05: "A proceeding shall be commenced by originating motion -- (a) where there
-- is no defendant to the proceeding; (b) where by or under any Act an application is authorised to be made to the Court;
-- or (c) where required by these Rules." r 4.06: "may be commenced by originating motion where -- (a) it is unlikely that
-- there will be any substantial dispute of fact; and (b) for that reason it is appropriate that there be no pleadings or
-- discovery."
-- An Act providing otherwise, a review under r 1.12 or an appeal under Order 58: NOTHING-kind *points outside the text*.
@export How must I start my proceeding in the Supreme Court: by writ or by originating motion (rr 4.01, 4.04 to 4.06)? A claim for damages against a defendant, where the facts are in dispute, is started by writ.
GIVEN `is there a defendant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is someone you are suing (a defendant)
      `does an Act authorise an application to the Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act authorises you to make an application to the Court (for example an application under a particular section)
      `do the Rules require an originating motion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another rule of these Rules requires this kind of proceeding to be started by originating motion
      `is a substantial dispute of fact unlikely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is unlikely that there will be any substantial dispute about the facts, so that no pleadings or discovery are needed
      `is it a review or an appeal from a lower court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is an order to review under the Administrative Law Act 1978 (r 1.12) or an appeal from a lower court or tribunal (Order 58)
      `does an Act say how it is begun` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act provides its own way of beginning this proceeding
GIVETH A MAYBE `How the proceeding is commenced`
`How must I start my proceeding` MEANS
    IF `is it a review or an appeal from a lower court` OR `does an Act say how it is begun`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF (NOT `is there a defendant`) OR `does an Act authorise an application to the Court` OR `do the Rules require an originating motion`
         THEN JUST `by originating motion`
         ELSE IF `is a substantial dispute of fact unlikely`
              THEN JUST `by writ, or by originating motion if I choose`
              ELSE JUST `by writ`

-- r 5.04(3): "An indorsement of claim on a writ shall constitute a statement of claim if, but only if, it is headed
-- "Statement of Claim"."
@export Is the claim written on my writ a statement of claim (r 5.04(3))? Only if it is headed "Statement of Claim"; if it is not, a separate statement of claim must be served after the defendant appears (r 14.02).
GIVEN `is it headed Statement of Claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the indorsement of claim on the writ is headed "Statement of Claim"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my writ's indorsement a statement of claim` MEANS
    IF `is it headed Statement of Claim`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `The trial the writ asks for` IS ONE OF
    `trial in Melbourne without a jury`
    `trial in Melbourne in the mode the writ states`
    `trial at the place the writ states without a jury`
    `trial at the place and in the mode the writ states`

-- r 5.08(1): "A writ shall be indorsed with a statement of the place and mode of trial desired." (2): "If the writ is not
-- indorsed with a statement as to the place of trial, the plaintiff shall be taken to desire trial in Melbourne, and, if
-- the writ is not indorsed with a statement as to the mode of trial, the plaintiff shall be taken to desire trial without
-- a jury."
@export If my writ does not say where or how I want my trial, what am I taken to want (r 5.08)? Trial in Melbourne, and without a jury.
GIVEN `does my writ state the place of trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your writ states the place of trial you want (Melbourne or a circuit town)
      `does my writ state the mode of trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your writ states the mode of trial you want (for example with a jury)
GIVETH A `The trial the writ asks for`
`What trial am I taken to want` MEANS
    IF `does my writ state the place of trial`
    THEN IF `does my writ state the mode of trial`
         THEN `trial at the place and in the mode the writ states`
         ELSE `trial at the place the writ states without a jury`
    ELSE IF `does my writ state the mode of trial`
         THEN `trial in Melbourne in the mode the writ states`
         ELSE `trial in Melbourne without a jury`

-- r 5.12(1): "A writ or an originating motion shall be valid for service for one year after the day it is filed." (2):
-- "Where a writ or originating motion has not been served on a defendant, the Court may, from time to time, by order
-- extend the period of validity for such period from the day of the order as the Court directs, being not more than one
-- year from that day." (3): before or after expiry. The year is counted by Order 3 (fork F1: r 3.04 adds the days from
-- 24 December to 9 January; finding 1), and a last day on a weekend or public holiday moves on (r 3.01(5)).
-- An extension of more than 12 months: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (beyond what r 5.12(2) allows).
GIVEN `the day it was filed`              IS A DATE
      `the Court extended it`             IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the extension order`    IS A DATE
      `the months of the extension`       IS A NUMBER
      `that last day is a public holiday` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`r 5.12 -- the last day the originating process may be served` `the day it was filed` `the Court extended it` `the day of the extension order` `the months of the extension` `that last day is a public holiday` MEANS
    IF `the Court extended it`
    THEN IF `the months of the extension` GREATER THAN 12
         THEN NOTHING
         ELSE JUST (`r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` `the months of the extension` `months after` `the day of the extension order` FALSE) `that last day is a public holiday`)
    ELSE JUST (`r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 12 `months after` `the day it was filed` FALSE) `that last day is a public holiday`)

@export Until what day can my writ (or originating motion) be served (r 5.12)? It is valid for service for one year after the day it was filed; if it has not been served, the Court may extend it, each time for up to one year from the day of its order.
GIVEN `the day my writ was filed` IS A DATE @desc The day the writ or originating motion was filed, as an ISO date
      `has the Court extended it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has made an order extending the writ's validity (answer no if not)
      `the day of the latest extension order` IS A DATE @desc If extended: the day of the latest extension order (any date if not extended)
      `how many months the Court extended it for` IS A NUMBER @desc If extended: the period of the extension in months (at most 12); 0 if not extended
      `is that last day a public holiday` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the day the validity would otherwise end on is a public holiday in Victoria (answer no if unsure)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`Until when can my writ be served` MEANS
    `r 5.12 -- the last day the originating process may be served` `the day my writ was filed` `has the Court extended it` `the day of the latest extension order` `how many months the Court extended it for` `is that last day a public holiday`

-- The same, asked of a day: outside the year (or the extension) JUST FALSE (a period the text fixes; ruling (a)).
@export Can my writ (or originating motion) still be served today, or has it expired (r 5.12)? If it has expired, the Court may still extend it, even after expiry, if it has not been served.
GIVEN `the day my writ was filed` IS A DATE @desc The day the writ or originating motion was filed, as an ISO date
      `has the Court extended it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has made an order extending the writ's validity
      `the day of the latest extension order` IS A DATE @desc If extended: the day of the latest extension order (any date if not extended)
      `how many months the Court extended it for` IS A NUMBER @desc If extended: the period of the extension in months (at most 12); 0 if not extended
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you plan to serve
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my writ still be served` MEANS
    CONSIDER `r 5.12 -- the last day the originating process may be served` `the day my writ was filed` `has the Court extended it` `the day of the latest extension order` `how many months the Court extended it for` FALSE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `the last day` THEN
        IF `today` `is on or before` `the last day`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Order 6 -- service`

-- r 6.02(1): "originating process shall be served personally on each defendant." (2): an unconditional appearance: taken
-- to be served personally on the day it is filed. r 6.03(1): personal service is "leaving a copy of the document with the
-- person to be served" or, if refused, "putting the copy down in the person's presence and telling the person the nature
-- of the document". r 6.05(1): "In any proceeding in which the plaintiff claims damages in respect of death or bodily
-- injury caused by or arising out of the use of a motor car, originating process shall not be taken to have been served on
-- the defendant unless a copy of the originating process is served on the defendant in accordance with these Rules and also
-- on the Transport Accident Commission." (2): the Court may order it taken to have been served. r 6.10: substituted
-- service by order. r 6.11: "Where ... a document has not been served in the manner required by these Rules, but the
-- document has come to the notice of the person to be served, the document shall be taken to have been served". r 6.14:
-- service as the parties agreed.
-- No way of service made out: JUST FALSE (r 6.02(1) "shall be served personally"; fork F7).
@export Has the defendant been served with my writ (rr 6.02, 6.03, 6.05, 6.10, 6.11, 6.14)? For a death or injury claim arising from the use of a motor car, the Transport Accident Commission must be served as well, unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `was a copy handed to the defendant personally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a sealed copy was left with the defendant, or put down in their presence after telling them what it was because they would not take it
      `has the defendant filed an unconditional appearance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has filed an unconditional appearance
      `was it served as a substituted service order directs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court made a substituted service order and its steps were taken, with the event or time it named having passed
      `has it come to the defendant's notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the writ has in fact come to the defendant's notice, even though not served the proper way
      `was it served as the parties agreed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was served in a way and at a place the parties agreed in advance
      `is it a motor car death or injury claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim damages for death or bodily injury caused by or arising out of the use of a motor car
      `was a copy served on the TAC` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a copy was also served on the Transport Accident Commission
      `has the Court ordered it taken as served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered that the writ be taken to have been served on the defendant although the Transport Accident Commission was not served
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Has the defendant been served with my writ` MEANS
    IF      `is it a motor car death or injury claim`
        AND (NOT `was a copy served on the TAC`)
        AND (NOT `has the Court ordered it taken as served`)
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF    `was a copy handed to the defendant personally`
            OR `has the defendant filed an unconditional appearance`
            OR `was it served as a substituted service order directs`
            OR `has it come to the defendant's notice`
            OR `was it served as the parties agreed`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `How the document was served` IS ONE OF
    `personally`
    `by email to the email address for service`
    `by leaving it at the proper address`
    `by post`
    `through a document exchange`
    `by a solicitor noting acceptance of service`

-- A served day moved by r 3.05(2) (a document other than originating process "served after 4.00 p.m. or on any day the
-- office of the Court is closed shall be taken to have been served on the next day the office is open") and r 6.07(4)
-- (email or leaving "effected after 4.00 p.m." is taken to have been effected "on the next day the Prothonotary's office is
-- open"). Public holidays not computed (fork F2).
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE, `after 4 pm` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DATE
`the open day of service for` `the day` `after 4 pm` MEANS
    IF `after 4 pm` OR `the day` `is a Saturday or Sunday`
    THEN `the next weekday after` `the day`
    ELSE `the day`

-- r 6.07(5): "The day of service of a document shall, where the document -- (a) is sent by post ... be taken to be the day it
-- would be delivered in the normal course of post; (b) is delivered into the facilities of a document exchange ... be taken
-- to be the day following the day upon which it is so delivered or, where a document is delivered on a Friday, be taken to
-- be the following Monday -- or on such other day as may be proved." r 6.09(2): a solicitor's note accepting service:
-- "the day the solicitor made the note". "Such other day as may be proved" is not computed (fork F8).
@export On what day is a document in my case taken to have been served (rr 3.05, 6.03, 6.07, 6.09)? Service by email or by leaving it after 4 pm counts from the next day the Court's office is open; a document (other than a writ) served on a weekend counts from the next open day; by post, the day it would arrive in the normal course of post; by document exchange, the next day (Monday if left on a Friday).
GIVEN `how it was served` IS A `How the document was served` @desc How the document was served: personally, by email to the email address for service, by leaving it at the proper address, by post, through a document exchange, or by a solicitor noting that they accept service
      `the day it was handed over, sent or left` IS A DATE @desc The day it was handed over, emailed, left, posted, put in the document exchange, or the solicitor made the note, as an ISO date
      `was it after 4 pm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was handed over, emailed or left after 4.00 pm
      `the day it would arrive by post` IS A DATE @desc If posted: the day it would be delivered in the normal course of post (any date if not posted)
      `is it the writ or other originating process` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document is the writ, originating motion or other originating process (rather than a later document in the case)
GIVETH A DATE
`On what day was the document served` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how it was served`
    WHEN `personally` THEN
        IF `is it the writ or other originating process`
        THEN `the day it was handed over, sent or left`
        ELSE `the open day of service for` `the day it was handed over, sent or left` `was it after 4 pm`
    WHEN `by a solicitor noting acceptance of service` THEN
        IF `is it the writ or other originating process`
        THEN `the day it was handed over, sent or left`
        ELSE `the open day of service for` `the day it was handed over, sent or left` `was it after 4 pm`
    WHEN `by email to the email address for service` THEN `the open day of service for` `the day it was handed over, sent or left` `was it after 4 pm`
    WHEN `by leaving it at the proper address` THEN `the open day of service for` `the day it was handed over, sent or left` `was it after 4 pm`
    WHEN `by post` THEN
        IF `is it the writ or other originating process`
        THEN `the day it would arrive by post`
        ELSE `the open day of service for` `the day it would arrive by post` FALSE
    WHEN `through a document exchange` THEN
        IF `is it the writ or other originating process`
        THEN `the exchange day`
        ELSE `the open day of service for` `the exchange day` FALSE
    WHERE
        `the exchange day` MEANS
            IF DATE_SERIAL `the day it was handed over, sent or left` MODULO 7 EQUALS 5
            THEN `the date` 3 `days after` `the day it was handed over, sent or left`
            ELSE `the date` 1 `days after` `the day it was handed over, sent or left`

DECLARE `Who may be served for me` IS ONE OF
    `a parent or guardian of mine`
    `the person I live with or who has care of me`
    `the person entitled to be my litigation guardian`

-- r 6.04: "Personal service of a document may be effected by serving the document in accordance with Rule 6.03, in the
-- case of -- ... (b) a minor, on a parent or guardian of the minor, and, if there is none, on the person with whom the
-- minor resides or in whose care the minor is; (c) a person under disability referred to in paragraph (b) of the definition
-- of that term in Rule 15.01, on -- (i) the person who, in accordance with Rule 15.03(2), would be entitled to be
-- litigation guardian ...; or (ii) if there is no such person, the person with whom that person under disability resides or
-- in whose care that person is".
-- An adult not incapable: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (r 6.04 adds ways of service; it does not exclude
-- serving the person).
@export If I am under 18, or unable to manage my affairs in the case, on whom may a document for me be personally served (r 6.04(b), (c))?
GIVEN `am I a minor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under 18
      `do I have a parent or guardian` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are under 18: whether you have a parent or guardian
      `am I unable to manage my affairs in the case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are incapable, because of injury, disease, senility, illness or physical or mental infirmity, of managing your affairs in the proceeding
      `is someone authorised by an Act to conduct cases for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person (for example an administrator or guardian appointed under an Act) is authorised by or under an Act to conduct legal proceedings in your name or for you
GIVETH A MAYBE `Who may be served for me`
`On whom may a document for me be served` MEANS
    IF `am I a minor`
    THEN IF `do I have a parent or guardian`
         THEN JUST `a parent or guardian of mine`
         ELSE JUST `the person I live with or who has care of me`
    ELSE IF `am I unable to manage my affairs in the case`
         THEN IF `is someone authorised by an Act to conduct cases for me`
              THEN JUST `the person entitled to be my litigation guardian`
              ELSE JUST `the person I live with or who has care of me`
         ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Order 7 and r 8.04 -- service out of Australia, and the time for appearance`

DECLARE `How I may serve out of Australia` IS ONE OF
    `without the Court's leave`
    `only with the Court's leave`

-- r 7.02: "An originating process may be served out of Australia without leave in the following cases -- (a) when the
-- claim is founded on a tortious act or omission -- (i) which was done or which occurred wholly or partly in Australia; or
-- (ii) in respect of which the damage was sustained wholly or partly in Australia; ... (g) when any relief is sought against
-- any person domiciled or ordinarily or habitually resident in Australia ...; (j) when the claim arises under an Australian
-- enactment and -- (i) any act or omission to which the claim relates was done or occurred in Australia; or (ii) any loss or
-- damage ... was sustained in Australia; ... (k) when the person to be served has submitted to the jurisdiction of the
-- Court; ... (n) when the claim is founded on a cause of action arising in Australia". The other heads ((b) to (f), (h),
-- (i), (l), (m), (o) to (s)) are asked as one fact. r 7.03(1): "when service is not allowed under Rule 7.02, an originating
-- process may be served out of Australia with the leave of the Court". r 7.01: the Part does not apply to service in New
-- Zealand under the Trans-Tasman Proceedings Act 2010 (Cth).
-- Service in New Zealand under that Act: NOTHING-kind *points outside the text*.
@export May I serve my writ on a defendant outside Australia without first getting the Court's leave (rr 7.01 to 7.03)? Yes for an injury claim where the wrong was done, or the damage suffered, wholly or partly in Australia, and in the other cases rule 7.02 lists; otherwise only with leave.
GIVEN `is it being served in New Zealand under the Trans-Tasman Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the writ is to be served in New Zealand under the Trans-Tasman Proceedings Act 2010 (Cth) (a different scheme)
      `was the wrong done wholly or partly in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is founded on a wrongful act or omission (a tort, such as negligence) done or occurring wholly or partly in Australia
      `was the damage suffered wholly or partly in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is founded on a tort and the damage (your injury or loss) was sustained wholly or partly in Australia
      `does the defendant live in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant is domiciled, or ordinarily or habitually resident, in Australia (even if now abroad)
      `is it a claim under an Australian law with acts or loss here` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim arises under an Australian Act and the act, omission, loss or damage it relates to happened in Australia
      `has the defendant submitted to the Court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has submitted to the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court of Victoria
      `did the cause of action arise in Australia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is founded on a cause of action arising in Australia
      `does another head of rule 7.02 apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another case listed in rule 7.02 applies (contracts, injunctions, property in Australia, trusts, necessary parties, estates, restitution, judgments, companies, arbitration, minors); answer no if unsure
GIVETH A MAYBE `How I may serve out of Australia`
`How may I serve my writ out of Australia` MEANS
    IF `is it being served in New Zealand under the Trans-Tasman Act`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF    `was the wrong done wholly or partly in Australia`
            OR `was the damage suffered wholly or partly in Australia`
            OR `does the defendant live in Australia`
            OR `is it a claim under an Australian law with acts or loss here`
            OR `has the defendant submitted to the Court`
            OR `did the cause of action arise in Australia`
            OR `does another head of rule 7.02 apply`
         THEN JUST `without the Court's leave`
         ELSE JUST `only with the Court's leave`

DECLARE `Where the writ is served` IS ONE OF
    `in Victoria`
    `elsewhere in Australia`
    `out of Australia`
    `in New Zealand under the Trans-Tasman Proceedings Act`

-- r 8.04: "Unless these Rules otherwise provide or the Court otherwise orders, the time stated in the writ ... for the
-- defendant to file an appearance shall be -- (a) where the originating process is to be served -- (i) in Victoria, not
-- less than 10 days after service; (ii) out of Victoria and in another part of Australia, 21 days after service; (b) ...
-- out of Australia, 42 days after service; (c) ... in New Zealand under Part 2 of the Trans-Tasman Proceedings Act 2010 ...
-- 30 working days". r 7.06: "Except when the Court otherwise orders, a defendant who has been served out of Australia shall
-- file an appearance within 42 days from the date of service."
-- New Zealand (working days under a Commonwealth Act), or a stated time under 10 days in Victoria: NOTHING-kind *points
-- outside the text* and *limited by its own wording*.
@export By what day must the defendant file an appearance to my writ (rr 8.04, 7.06, 3.01)? In Victoria the writ states a time of at least 10 days after service; elsewhere in Australia it is 21 days; out of Australia, 42 days. If the defendant does not appear in time I may seek judgment in default (Order 21).
GIVEN `the day the writ was served` IS A DATE @desc The day the writ was served on the defendant, as an ISO date
      `where it was served` IS A `Where the writ is served` @desc Where the writ was served: in Victoria, elsewhere in Australia, out of Australia, or in New Zealand under the Trans-Tasman Proceedings Act
      `the days the writ states` IS A NUMBER @desc If served in Victoria: the number of days after service the writ gives for appearance (at least 10; the usual form states 10)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must the defendant appear` MEANS
    CONSIDER `where it was served`
    WHEN `in Victoria` THEN
        IF `the days the writ states` LESS THAN 10
        THEN NOTHING
        ELSE JUST (`the appearance day for` `the days the writ states`)
    WHEN `elsewhere in Australia` THEN JUST (`the appearance day for` 21)
    WHEN `out of Australia` THEN JUST (`the appearance day for` 42)
    WHEN `in New Zealand under the Trans-Tasman Proceedings Act` THEN NOTHING
    WHERE
        `the appearance day for` n MEANS `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` n `days after` `the day the writ was served` FALSE) FALSE

§§ `Order 13 -- pleadings`

-- r 13.10(4): "The pleading of a party who claims damages for bodily injury shall state -- (a) particulars, with dates and
-- amounts, of all earnings lost in consequence of the injury complained of; (b) particulars of any loss of earning capacity
-- resulting from the injury; (c) the date of the party's birth; (d) the name and address of each of the party's employers
-- commencing from the day being 12 months before the party sustained the injury, the time of commencement and the duration
-- of each employment and the total net amount, after deduction of tax, that was earned in each employment."
-- r 1.13: "bodily injury includes any impairment of mental condition or disease".
-- Not a claim for damages for bodily injury: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Must my statement of claim for an injury give particulars of my lost earnings, my lost earning capacity, my date of birth and my employers (r 13.10(4))? It must, if I claim damages for bodily injury (which includes a mental impairment or disease).
GIVEN `do I claim damages for bodily injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim damages for bodily injury (including any impairment of mental condition, or disease)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my pleading give my earnings and employment particulars` MEANS
    IF `do I claim damages for bodily injury`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

@export From what day must my statement of claim for an injury list my employers and what I earned with each (r 13.10(4)(d))? From the day 12 months before I was injured.
GIVEN `do I claim damages for bodily injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim damages for bodily injury (including any impairment of mental condition, or disease)
      `the day I was injured` IS A DATE @desc The day you sustained the injury, as an ISO date
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`From when must I list my employers` MEANS
    IF `do I claim damages for bodily injury`
    THEN JUST (`the date` (0 MINUS 12) `months after` `the day I was injured`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- r 13.07(3): "A claim for exemplary damages shall be specifically pleaded together with the facts on which the party
-- pleading relies."
-- No claim for exemplary damages: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export If I claim exemplary (punitive) damages, must I plead that claim specifically, with the facts I rely on (r 13.07(3))?
GIVEN `do I claim exemplary damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you claim exemplary damages (damages to punish the defendant's conduct)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I plead exemplary damages specifically` MEANS
    IF `do I claim exemplary damages`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

-- r 13.12(1): "every allegation of fact in any pleading shall be taken to be admitted unless it is denied specifically or by
-- necessary implication or is stated to be not admitted in the pleading of the opposite party, or unless a joinder of
-- issue under Rule 13.13 operates as a denial of it". (3): "Any allegation that a party has suffered damage and any
-- allegation as to the amount of damages shall be taken to be denied unless specifically admitted." r 15.06:
-- "Notwithstanding Rule 13.12(1), a person under disability shall not be taken to admit the truth of any allegation of fact
-- made in the pleading of the opposite party unless in his or her pleading the person under disability states that the
-- allegation is admitted."
@export Am I taken to admit an allegation of fact in the other side's pleading (rr 13.12, 13.13, 15.06)? Yes, unless I deny it (specifically or by necessary implication), say it is not admitted, or an implied joinder of issue denies it; but an allegation about damage or the amount of damages is taken to be denied unless I specifically admit it, and a person under disability admits only what they expressly admit.
GIVEN `is it about damage suffered or the amount of damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the allegation is that a party has suffered damage, or is about the amount of damages
      `did I state in my pleading that it is admitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your pleading expressly states that the allegation is admitted
      `did I deny it or say it is not admitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your pleading denies it, specifically or by necessary implication, or states that it is not admitted (a general denial is not enough)
      `does a joinder of issue deny it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a joinder of issue operates as a denial of it (at the close of pleadings issue is implied on the last pleading served, but never on a statement of claim or counterclaim)
      `am I a person under disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a minor, or incapable of managing your affairs in the proceeding (and so sue or defend by a litigation guardian)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I taken to admit the allegation` MEANS
    IF `did I state in my pleading that it is admitted`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF `am I a person under disability`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `is it about damage suffered or the amount of damages`
              THEN JUST FALSE
              ELSE IF `did I deny it or say it is not admitted` OR `does a joinder of issue deny it`
                   THEN JUST FALSE
                   ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `Order 14 -- serving the pleadings`

-- r 14.01: "Where the indorsement of claim on a writ constitutes a statement of claim ... no statement of claim shall be
-- served." r 14.02: otherwise, where a defendant files an appearance, "the plaintiff shall serve a statement of claim on
-- that defendant within 30 days after the defendant's appearance, unless the Court otherwise orders."
-- The writ's indorsement already the statement of claim, or the Court has ordered another time: NOTHING-kind *limited by
-- its own wording* / *points outside the text* (the order).
GIVEN `the indorsement is a statement of claim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the defendant appeared` IS A DATE
      `the Court ordered another time` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`r 14.02 -- the last day for the statement of claim` `the indorsement is a statement of claim` `the day the defendant appeared` `the Court ordered another time` MEANS
    IF `the indorsement is a statement of claim` OR `the Court ordered another time`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 30 `days after` `the day the defendant appeared` FALSE) FALSE)

@export By what day must I serve my statement of claim on a defendant who has filed an appearance (rr 14.01, 14.02)? Within 30 days after the appearance, unless the Court orders otherwise; none is served where the claim on the writ is headed "Statement of Claim". Missing it can lead to dismissal for want of prosecution (r 24.01).
GIVEN `is the claim on my writ headed Statement of Claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the indorsement of claim on your writ is headed "Statement of Claim"
      `the day the defendant appeared` IS A DATE @desc The day the defendant filed an appearance, as an ISO date
      `has the Court ordered another time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered a different time (answer no if not)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must I serve my statement of claim` MEANS
    `r 14.02 -- the last day for the statement of claim` `is the claim on my writ headed Statement of Claim` `the day the defendant appeared` `has the Court ordered another time`

@export Have I served my statement of claim in time, or am I still in time (r 14.02)? The duty runs 30 days from the defendant's appearance.
GIVEN `is the claim on my writ headed Statement of Claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the indorsement of claim on your writ is headed "Statement of Claim"
      `the day the defendant appeared` IS A DATE @desc The day the defendant filed an appearance, as an ISO date
      `has the Court ordered another time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered a different time (answer no if not)
      `have I served it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have served the statement of claim
      `the day I served it` IS A DATE @desc If served: the day it was served (any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A MAYBE `What became of my step`
`Was my statement of claim served in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER `r 14.02 -- the last day for the statement of claim` `is the claim on my writ headed Statement of Claim` `the day the defendant appeared` `has the Court ordered another time`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    WHEN JUST `the last day` THEN JUST (`what became of the step` `serve the statement of claim` `the day the defendant appeared` `the last day` `have I served it` `the day I served it` `today`)

-- r 14.04: "In a proceeding commenced by writ, a defendant who files an appearance shall serve a defence as follows -- (a)
-- where the indorsement of claim on the writ constitutes a statement of claim ..., within 30 days after filing the
-- appearance; (b) where the plaintiff serves a statement of claim, within 30 days after service of the statement of claim;
-- or (c) within such time as the Court directs."
GIVEN `the indorsement is a statement of claim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the appearance`               IS A DATE
      `the day the statement of claim was served` IS A DATE
      `the Court directed a time`               IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the Court directed`              IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`r 14.04 -- the last day for the defence` `the indorsement is a statement of claim` `the day of the appearance` `the day the statement of claim was served` `the Court directed a time` `the day the Court directed` MEANS
    IF `the Court directed a time`
    THEN `the day the Court directed`
    ELSE IF `the indorsement is a statement of claim`
         THEN `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 30 `days after` `the day of the appearance` FALSE) FALSE
         ELSE `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 30 `days after` `the day the statement of claim was served` FALSE) FALSE

-- The day the time for the defence starts (for the regulative rule).
GIVEN `the indorsement is a statement of claim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the appearance`               IS A DATE
      `the day the statement of claim was served` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`r 14.04 -- the day the time for the defence starts` `the indorsement is a statement of claim` `the day of the appearance` `the day the statement of claim was served` MEANS
    IF `the indorsement is a statement of claim`
    THEN `the day of the appearance`
    ELSE `the day the statement of claim was served`

@export By what day must the defence be served in my case (r 14.04)? Within 30 days after the defendant's appearance where the writ's claim is the statement of claim, otherwise within 30 days after the statement of claim is served, or by the day the Court directs. If it is not, the plaintiff may seek judgment in default of defence (r 21.02).
GIVEN `is the claim on the writ headed Statement of Claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the indorsement of claim on the writ is headed "Statement of Claim"
      `the day the defendant appeared` IS A DATE @desc The day the defendant filed an appearance, as an ISO date
      `the day the statement of claim was served` IS A DATE @desc If a separate statement of claim was served: the day it was served (any date otherwise)
      `has the Court directed a time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has directed a time for the defence
      `the day the Court directed` IS A DATE @desc If so: the last day the Court directed (any date otherwise)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must the defence be served` MEANS
    `r 14.04 -- the last day for the defence` `is the claim on the writ headed Statement of Claim` `the day the defendant appeared` `the day the statement of claim was served` `has the Court directed a time` `the day the Court directed`

@export Was the defence served in time, or is the defendant still in time (r 14.04)? Asked by either side: a defendant who must serve it, or a plaintiff deciding whether to seek default judgment.
GIVEN `is the claim on the writ headed Statement of Claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the indorsement of claim on the writ is headed "Statement of Claim"
      `the day the defendant appeared` IS A DATE @desc The day the defendant filed an appearance, as an ISO date
      `the day the statement of claim was served` IS A DATE @desc If a separate statement of claim was served: the day it was served (any date otherwise)
      `has the Court directed a time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has directed a time for the defence
      `the day the Court directed` IS A DATE @desc If so: the last day the Court directed (any date otherwise)
      `has the defence been served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defence has been served
      `the day it was served` IS A DATE @desc If served: the day it was served (any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What became of my step`
`Was the defence served in time` MEANS
    `what became of the step` `serve the defence` (`r 14.04 -- the day the time for the defence starts` `is the claim on the writ headed Statement of Claim` `the day the defendant appeared` `the day the statement of claim was served`) (`r 14.04 -- the last day for the defence` `is the claim on the writ headed Statement of Claim` `the day the defendant appeared` `the day the statement of claim was served` `has the Court directed a time` `the day the Court directed`) `has the defence been served` `the day it was served` `today`

-- r 14.05: "Where the plaintiff is required to serve a reply, it shall be served within 30 days after service of the
-- defence, unless the Court otherwise orders." r 14.07: a reply and defence to counterclaim, or a defence to counterclaim,
-- "within 30 days after service of the defence and counterclaim, unless the Court otherwise orders."
@export By what day must I serve my reply, or my defence to the defendant's counterclaim (rr 14.05, 14.07)? Within 30 days after the defence (and counterclaim) is served, unless the Court orders otherwise.
GIVEN `the day the defence was served` IS A DATE @desc The day the defence (or defence and counterclaim) was served on you, as an ISO date
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I serve my reply` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 30 `days after` `the day the defence was served` FALSE) FALSE

-- r 14.08: "Unless the Court otherwise orders, pleadings shall be closed -- (a) where no pleading beyond a defence is
-- ordered or served, at the expiration of 30 days after service of the defence; (b) where pleadings beyond a defence are
-- ordered or served, at the expiration of 30 days after service of the last of those pleadings." The answer is the 30th
-- day: the pleadings close at its end (r 3.01(5) does not move it: closing is not an act).
@export On what day do the pleadings in my case close (r 14.08)? At the end of the 30th day after the defence was served, or after the last pleading beyond the defence. A notice for discovery can be served once they close (r 29.02).
GIVEN `the day the defence was served` IS A DATE @desc The day the defence was served, as an ISO date
      `was any pleading beyond the defence ordered or served` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a reply or other pleading after the defence was ordered or served
      `the day the last pleading was served` IS A DATE @desc If so: the day the last of those pleadings was served (any date otherwise)
GIVETH A DATE
`When do the pleadings close` MEANS
    IF `was any pleading beyond the defence ordered or served`
    THEN `the last counted day of` 30 `days after` `the day the last pleading was served` FALSE
    ELSE `the last counted day of` 30 `days after` `the day the defence was served` FALSE

§§ `Order 15 -- persons under disability`

-- r 15.01: "In this Order, person under disability means -- (a) a minor; or (b) a person who is incapable, by reason of
-- injury, disease, senility, illness or physical or mental infirmity, of managing that person's affairs in relation to the
-- proceeding." ("minor" is borrowed from the Age of Majority Act 1977: asked.)
GIVEN `a minor` IS A BOOLEAN, `incapable of managing affairs in the proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `r 15.01 -- a person under disability` `a minor` `incapable of managing affairs in the proceeding` IF
    `a minor` OR `incapable of managing affairs in the proceeding`

@export Am I a "person under disability" for the Supreme Court Rules (r 15.01)? A minor (under 18), or a person who, because of injury, disease, senility, illness or physical or mental infirmity, cannot manage their affairs in the case. Such a person sues or defends through a litigation guardian.
GIVEN `am I a minor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under 18
      `am I unable to manage my affairs in the case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are incapable, because of injury, disease, senility, illness or physical or mental infirmity, of managing your affairs in the proceeding
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I a person under disability` MEANS
    IF `r 15.01 -- a person under disability` `am I a minor` `am I unable to manage my affairs in the case`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 15.02(1): "Except where otherwise provided by or under any Act, a person under disability shall commence or defend a
-- proceeding by the person's litigation guardian." (3): "A litigation guardian of a person under disability shall act by a
-- solicitor."
-- Not under disability: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*. An Act providing otherwise: NOTHING-kind *points
-- outside the text*.
@export Must I bring (or defend) my case through a litigation guardian (r 15.02)? A person under disability must, unless an Act provides otherwise, and the litigation guardian must act through a solicitor.
GIVEN `am I a minor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are under 18
      `am I unable to manage my affairs in the case` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are incapable, because of injury, disease, senility, illness or physical or mental infirmity, of managing your affairs in the proceeding
      `does an Act let me sue without one` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Act provides that you may bring or defend this proceeding without a litigation guardian (answer no if unsure)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I act through a litigation guardian` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`r 15.01 -- a person under disability` `am I a minor` `am I unable to manage my affairs in the case`))
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `does an Act let me sue without one`
         THEN NOTHING
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- r 15.03(1): "A person may be a litigation guardian of a person under disability if the first-mentioned person -- (a) is
-- not a person under disability; and (b) has no interest in the proceeding adverse to that of the person under
-- disability." (5): "Where a party has a litigation guardian in a proceeding, no other person shall act as litigation
-- guardian, unless the Court otherwise orders." (6): "Except where a litigation guardian has been appointed by the Court,
-- the name of a person shall not be used in a proceeding as litigation guardian ... unless there is first filed in the
-- office of the Prothonotary -- (a) the written consent of the person to be the litigation guardian; and (b) a certificate
-- by the solicitor for the person under disability" (that the person is under disability, and the guardian has consented
-- and has no adverse interest).
@export May this person act as my litigation guardian (r 15.03)? They must not be under disability themselves, must have no interest in the case against mine, and (unless the Court appoints them) their written consent and my solicitor's certificate must first be filed; if I already have a litigation guardian, no one else may act unless the Court orders.
GIVEN `is the person under disability themselves` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proposed litigation guardian is under 18, or unable to manage their own affairs
      `does the person have an interest against mine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proposed litigation guardian has an interest in the proceeding adverse to yours (for example a claim against the same insurance money)
      `has the Court appointed the person` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has appointed the person as your litigation guardian
      `are the consent and solicitor's certificate filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person's written consent and your solicitor's certificate have been filed with the Prothonotary
      `do I already have another litigation guardian` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone else is already your litigation guardian in the proceeding
      `has the Court ordered that the person may act anyway` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you already have one: whether the Court has ordered that this person may act
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May this person be my litigation guardian` MEANS
    IF `is the person under disability themselves` OR `does the person have an interest against mine`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `do I already have another litigation guardian` AND (NOT `has the Court ordered that the person may act anyway`)
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE IF `has the Court appointed the person` OR `are the consent and solicitor's certificate filed`
              THEN JUST TRUE
              ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 15.02(4): "Unless for special reason the Court otherwise orders, the litigation guardian of a person under disability
-- is not personally liable for costs awarded against the person."
@export Is my litigation guardian personally liable for costs awarded against me (r 15.02(4))? No, unless the Court orders so for a special reason.
GIVEN `has the Court ordered so for special reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has ordered, for a special reason, that the litigation guardian pay the costs personally
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my litigation guardian liable for costs against me` MEANS
    IF `has the Court ordered so for special reason`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 15.08(1): "Where in a proceeding a claim is made by or on behalf of or against a person under disability, no
-- compromise, payment of money or acceptance of an offer of compromise under Order 26, whenever entered into or made,
-- shall so far as it relates to that claim be valid without the approval of the Court." r 26.06: "no acceptance ... shall
-- be binding until the Court has approved the compromise." (5): an approved acceptance is taken to be made "at the time
-- of approval".
-- A claim not by or against a person under disability: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Is the settlement of my claim valid if I am a minor or cannot manage my affairs (rr 15.08(1), 26.06)? Not without the Court's approval: no compromise, payment or accepted offer of compromise is valid or binding until the Court approves it.
GIVEN `is the claim by or against a person under disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is made by, for or against a minor, or a person unable to manage their affairs in the proceeding
      `has the Court approved the settlement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has approved the compromise, payment or acceptance
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the settlement valid` MEANS
    IF (NOT `is the claim by or against a person under disability`)
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `has the Court approved the settlement`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 15.08(2): "Application for approval shall be by summons filed not later than 30 days after the compromise, payment or
-- acceptance." (3): "The Court may dispense with the requirement of a summons where application for approval is made at
-- the trial of the proceeding."
-- Approval sought at the trial: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (the summons may be dispensed with).
GIVEN `the day of the compromise` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`r 15.08(2) -- the last day for the approval summons` `the day of the compromise` MEANS
    `r 3.01(5) -- the day the act may be done for a last day of` (`the last counted day of` 30 `days after` `the day of the compromise` FALSE) FALSE

@export By what day must the summons for the Court's approval of a settlement for a minor or a person under disability be filed (r 15.08(2), (3))? Not later than 30 days after the compromise, payment or acceptance; at the trial the Court may dispense with a summons.
GIVEN `the day of the settlement` IS A DATE @desc The day of the compromise, payment or acceptance of the offer, as an ISO date
      `is approval being sought at the trial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether approval is being sought at the trial of the proceeding (where the Court may dispense with a summons)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must the approval summons be filed` MEANS
    IF `is approval being sought at the trial`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`r 15.08(2) -- the last day for the approval summons` `the day of the settlement`)

@export Was the summons for approval of my settlement filed in time, or is there still time (r 15.08(2))?
GIVEN `the day of the settlement` IS A DATE @desc The day of the compromise, payment or acceptance of the offer, as an ISO date
      `has the summons been filed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the summons for approval has been filed
      `the day it was filed` IS A DATE @desc If filed: the day it was filed (any date if not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What became of my step`
`Was the approval summons filed in time` MEANS
    `what became of the step` `file the summons for approval of the compromise` `the day of the settlement` (`r 15.08(2) -- the last day for the approval summons` `the day of the settlement`) `has the summons been filed` `the day it was filed` `today`

-- r 15.04: "Where a defendant who is a person under disability does not file an appearance within the time limited, the
-- plaintiff shall not continue the proceeding unless a person -- (a) is made litigation guardian of the defendant in
-- accordance with Rule 15.03(6); or (b) is appointed litigation guardian by order of the Court."
-- A defendant not under disability, or one who appeared: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Can the case go on against a defendant who is a minor or unable to manage their affairs and who has not filed an appearance (r 15.04)? Only once someone becomes, or the Court appoints someone as, that defendant's litigation guardian.
GIVEN `is the defendant under disability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant is a minor, or unable to manage their affairs in the proceeding
      `has the defendant appeared in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant filed an appearance within the time limited
      `does the defendant have a litigation guardian` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone has become the defendant's litigation guardian (consent and certificate filed) or been appointed by the Court
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the case continue against the defendant under disability` MEANS
    IF (NOT `is the defendant under disability`) OR `has the defendant appeared in time`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE IF `does the defendant have a litigation guardian`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `Order 18A -- group proceedings (with Supreme Court Act 1986 ss 33E, 33J restated)`

DECLARE `Kind of person` IS ONE OF
    `an individual or company`
    `the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory`
    `a Minister of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory`
    `a public-purpose statutory body corporate`
    `a judge, magistrate or other judicial officer`
    `another officer of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory, in that capacity`

-- Restated narrowly from the Supreme Court Act 1986 (the parent Act, which a module here cannot import): s 33E(1): "the
-- consent of a person to be a group member is not required." (2): "None of the following persons is a group member unless
-- the person gives consent in writing to being so -- (a) the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory; or (b) a Minister ...;
-- (c) a body corporate established for a public purpose by a law of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory, other than
-- an incorporated company or association; or (d) any judge, magistrate or other judicial officer ...; or (e) any other
-- officer ..., in his or her capacity as an officer." r 18A.02: "A consent in writing under section 33E(2) of the Act shall
-- be in Form 18AA."
@export Must I give written consent, in Form 18AA, to be a member of a group proceeding (class action) in the Supreme Court (r 18A.02; Supreme Court Act 1986 s 33E)? An ordinary individual or company needs no consent; only governments, Ministers, public bodies, judicial officers and officers acting as such must consent in writing, in Form 18AA.
GIVEN `what kind of person am I` IS A `Kind of person` @desc What kind of person you are: an individual or company (the usual case), the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory, a Minister, a public-purpose statutory body, a judicial officer, or another government officer acting as such
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I consent in Form 18AA to be a group member` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what kind of person am I`
    WHEN `an individual or company` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- Restated narrowly: s 33J(1): "The Court must fix a date before which a group member may opt out of a group proceeding."
-- (2): "A group member may opt out of the group proceeding by notice in writing before the date so fixed." (3): the Court
-- may extend the period. r 18A.04: "A notice in writing under section 33J(2) of the Act shall be in Form 18AB."
-- "Before the date so fixed": a notice given on that date is not before it (fork F9).
@export Is my notice opting out of a group proceeding (class action) good (r 18A.04; Supreme Court Act 1986 s 33J)? It must be in writing, in Form 18AB, and given before the opt-out date the Court fixed (or extended).
GIVEN `is my notice in writing in Form 18AB` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your opt-out notice is in writing in Form 18AB
      `the opt-out date the Court fixed` IS A DATE @desc The date fixed (or as extended) by the Court before which group members may opt out, as an ISO date
      `the day I gave the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave (or will give) the notice
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my opt-out notice good` MEANS
    IF `is my notice in writing in Form 18AB` AND `the day I gave the notice` `is before` `the opt-out date the Court fixed`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- r 18A.05(1): "This Rule applies to an application for an order under section 33K, 33W, 33X or 33ZA of the Act in
-- relation to which the Court may require notice to be given to group members." (2): "The application shall be supported
-- by an affidavit setting out to the best of the applicant's knowledge, information and belief -- (a) the identity or
-- description of the group members; (b) the whereabouts of the group members; and (c) the means by which a notice ordered
-- by the Court is most likely to come to the attention of the group members."
-- Another application: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export As the plaintiff in a group proceeding, must my application be supported by an affidavit about who and where the group members are and how a notice will best reach them (r 18A.05)? Yes, for an application under section 33K, 33W, 33X or 33ZA of the Supreme Court Act 1986 on which the Court may require notice to group members (for example to approve a settlement).
GIVEN `is it an application under s 33K, 33W, 33X or 33ZA` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your application is for an order under section 33K (claims accruing later), 33W (settling an individual claim), 33X (notices) or 33ZA (a fund) of the Supreme Court Act 1986
      `may the Court require notice to group members` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is an application on which the Court may require notice to be given to group members
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my application have a group member affidavit` MEANS
    IF `is it an application under s 33K, 33W, 33X or 33ZA` AND `may the Court require notice to group members`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING
